WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.000
He hasn't been in the ring with Rocky in awhile,
but he's still been pretty busy.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.660
Here's what Carl Weathers has been up to since
his iconic turn as Apollo Creed.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.820
"What do you think you know about me?"

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.739
Weathers poked fun at his most famous post-Apollo
role on Saturday Night Live in 2003, reminding

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.500
everyone that he will indeed be forever remembered
in the popular imagination as "the black guy

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.000
in Predator."

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.039
1987's Predator was first and foremost an
Arnold Schwarzenegger film, but it was a big

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:29.710
deal for Weathers at the time, proving he
could be a formidable presence on the big

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.340
screen with or without Sylvester Stallone
at his side, which is likely why he soon found

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.680
himself headlining his own action film.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.660
1988's Action Jackson could have launched
Weathers into superstardom as the first film

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.650
in a lucrative action franchise … but the
film's failing production company was sold

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.080
off, and the film was universally panned,
primarily for dialogue like this:

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.400
"Barbeque, huh?

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.360
How do you like your ribs?"

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.180
But Weathers had high hopes: he told Bullz-Eye
in 2009,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.200
"Yeah, people loved Action Jackson.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.630
I get asked all the time, 'Are you ever going
to do a sequel?'

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.580
I still maintain today that it could've been
a better movie had we taken a little bit longer

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.040
to develop the screenplay, but a lot of people
liked it.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.620
In Germany, it was huge!"

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:16.520
Weathers tried his hand at being an action
star once again in 1992's Hurricane Smith,

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.470
think a low-rent Action Jackson set in Australia,
but it wasn't huge anywhere, and it's best

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.560
summed up by the alternate international title:
Dead on Delivery.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.500
Weathers wasn't necessarily known for his
comedy skills when his agent steered him toward

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.480
Adam Sandler in 1996.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.080
But Weathers soon found himself in Sandler's
Happy Gilmore playing Chubbs Peterson, a former

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.040
pro golfer who was forced into retirement
after an alligator bit off his hand.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.680
"I tore one of that bastard's eyes out, though.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:43.600
Look at that."

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:45.880
"You're pretty sick, Chubbs."

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.660
The performance showed the world a different
side of Weathers, which led to further comedy

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.080
roles, including on one of the best sitcoms
of all time ...

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.580
Although he's only appeared in a grand total
of four episodes over the course of the five

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.130
seasons (so far) of Arrested Development,
Weathers relished the opportunity to get a

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.200
little goofy by playing an exaggerated version
of himself.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.360
"Whoa whoa whoa whoa whoa.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.720
There's still plenty of meat on that bone.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.660
You take this home, throw it in a pot, add
some broth, a potato, baby, you've got a stew

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.160
going!"

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.860
Series creator Mitchell Hurwitz had originally
contemplated going in a different direction

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.340
with Bizarro Carl, one which would've involved
a parody of Rocky and Apollo's beach run in

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.319
Rocky III, but when he asked Weathers if he
had any thoughts, Weathers suggested that

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.320
maybe he could be really cheap instead.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.660
Hurwitz told Vulture,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.980
"It was so much better.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.159
I went back to the writers room and said,
'You're not going to believe this.

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:36.230
Carl Weathers wants to be incredibly cheap.'

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.760
All credit to Carl on that."

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.439
After his career in the NFL, but before he
became King of the Morning Shows, Michael

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.100
Strahan took a shot at sitcom stardom with
FOX's Brothers.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.799
Weathers played Strahan's dad, and told the
Times-Picayune in advance of the show's September

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.019
2009 premiere,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.671
"You see a contemporary black family that
expresses itself with a bit of edge, it's

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.520
an edgy show, but at the same time you see
the love."

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.099
Unfortunately, the love wasn't shared by the
viewing public: by the end of December, FOX

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.720
had already bid adieu to the Brothers.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.520
If you're an old-school cable box channel
surfer, you've likely spotted Weathers popping

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:15.069
up on plenty of shows, from voice work on
the Cartoon Network, to character roles and

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:17.400
award show appearances all across the dial.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.180
After spending a season playing Josh Holloway's
partner on USA's Colony, Weathers added a

00:03:22.189 --> 00:03:27.840
new series-regular credit to his name in 2017:
introduced as State's Attorney Mark Jefferies

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.519
in an episode of NBC's Chicago P.D., he reprised
the role on Chicago Justice, and an episode

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.980
of Law &amp; Order: SVU.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.440
That isn't the only role keeping him busy
lately, either, Weathers also popped up in

00:03:39.449 --> 00:03:43.399
an early episode of CBS's rebooted Magnum
P.I. in 2018.

